/// \defgroup grp_w1020_wwlib_mpmath_bits_primes WWLib big-number helpers: the prime table, bits, digits and signs
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief The small XMP_ routines in mpmath.cpp that the arithmetic,
///        encoding and primality code is built from, plus three helpers
///        declared only in mpmath.cpp.
///
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath explains the number format (an array of
/// 32-bit digits, least significant first, in two's complement, with the
/// digit count passed last) and who uses this code.
/// \ref grp_w1012_wwlib_mpmath_arith and
/// \ref grp_w1012_wwlib_mpmath_encode_prime cover the larger routines. The
/// inline helpers (see below) are documented on the \ref mpmath.h
/// "mpmath.h" file page; the other entries listed here are on the
/// \ref mpmath.cpp "mpmath.cpp" file page:
/// - setup and copy: XMP_Init and XMP_Move;
/// - in-place increment, decrement, complement and absolute value:
///   XMP_Inc, XMP_Dec, XMP_Not and XMP_Abs;
/// - queries: XMP_Significance, XMP_Test_Eq_Int, XMP_Is_Negative and
///   XMP_Count_Bytes;
/// - bit and unit conversions: XMP_Set_Bit, XMP_Test_Bit,
///   XMP_Bits_To_Mask, XMP_Bits_To_Digits and XMP_Digits_To_Bits;
/// - the prime table accessors XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table and
///   XMP_Fetch_Prime_Size;
/// - the helpers declared only in mpmath.cpp: _Byte_Precision, which is
///   static, and memrev and pfunc, which are not.
///
/// mpmath.cpp also holds XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test, which no retail code
/// calls; the Retail quirks list on the \ref mpmath.cpp "mpmath.cpp" file
/// page describes it.
///
/// "int.h" on this page means Zero Hour's WWLib int.h. BFME 2 has no copy
/// in its own tree; pk.cpp reaches the Open-BFME-1 reference copy through
/// pk.h, and that copy has the same code.
///
/// \par Inline helpers and their retail copies
/// XMP_Set_Bit, XMP_Test_Bit, XMP_Bits_To_Mask, XMP_Bits_To_Digits,
/// XMP_Digits_To_Bits and XMP_Is_Negative are declared extern "C" in
/// mpmath.h and also defined there as inline functions. Retail still holds
/// an out-of-line copy of each. Most routines in this group have no direct
/// call and no stored address anywhere in the retail image, so wherever a
/// retail body uses one of them, the call has been compiled inline. The
/// int.h wrappers are template members, which emit code only where they
/// are instantiated, so a source-level caller in int.h is not evidence of a
/// retail caller. The exceptions are XMP_Init, whose retail callers include
/// Int<64> and PKey code (see its entry); the two prime table accessors,
/// which RemainderTable calls; XMP_Bits_To_Mask and XMP_Significance,
/// which XMP_Exponent_Mod calls; and pfunc, whose address
/// XMP_Is_Small_Prime passes to bsearch. The caller lists in the entries
/// are source-level unless they say otherwise.
///
/// All of these bodies are the same as in Zero Hour's mpmath.cpp and
/// mpmath.h.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here is data-driven. The prime table, the 32-bit digit size and
/// the MAX_UNIT_PRECISION limit of 64 digits are compile-time constants.
/// The game is not known to reach this code (see
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath), so a change here is not known to affect
/// lockstep sync, saves or replays.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The bit helpers hard-code a 32-bit digit: UNITSIZE is 32 and
///   LOG_UNITSIZE is 5, while digit is a typedef for unsigned long. A
///   platform with a 64-bit unsigned long breaks them unchanged.
/// - XMP_Count_Bytes walks the digit array byte by byte in memory order,
///   which gives the low-order bytes first only on a little-endian machine.
/// - memrev is not reached from any code in the retail image, so a port
///   can leave it out.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/mpmath.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_mpmath_bits_primes
/// \brief The extern "C" declarations of the XMP_ routines, the digit
///        type and precision constants they share, and six small inline
///        bit and sign helpers.
///
/// Zero Hour's WWLib MPMATH.H. BFME 2 has no copy in its own tree:
/// mpmath.cpp includes the Open-BFME-1 reference copy, and pk.cpp reaches
/// it through pk.h and int.h. It defines:
/// - digit, a typedef for unsigned long, and signeddigit, a macro for
///   signed long;
/// - UNITSIZE (32 bits per digit) and LOG_UNITSIZE (5, its base-2
///   logarithm);
/// - MAX_BIT_PRECISION (2,048) and MAX_UNIT_PRECISION (64 digits), the
///   ceiling that the routines size their scratch numbers from;
/// - MPEXPORT, which expands to nothing.
///
/// The routines declared here are defined in mpmath.cpp, and their entries
/// are with that file. The inline helpers XMP_Digits_To_Bits,
/// XMP_Bits_To_Digits, XMP_Bits_To_Mask, XMP_Is_Negative, XMP_Set_Bit and
/// XMP_Test_Bit are defined in this header, and their entries are on this
/// page. Zero Hour's int.h builds the Int template on all of it.

/// \fn unsigned short const *XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table(void)
/// \brief Returns the table of small primes: every prime from 2 to 32719,
///        in ascending order.
///
/// The table, primeTable, is defined in mpmath.cpp and holds 3,511
/// entries, a count that XMP_Fetch_Prime_Size reports. The routines in
/// mpmath.cpp read the array directly: XMP_Small_Divisors_Test divides by
/// every entry, XMP_Fermat_Test takes its bases from the start, and
/// XMP_Is_Small_Prime searches it. Code outside the file goes through this
/// accessor. In retail its direct callers are Int<64>'s RemainderTable
/// constructor and the RemainderTable::Increment overload that takes an
/// unsigned short. RemainderTable keeps a number's
/// remainders modulo every table prime so that Generate_Prime can skip
/// candidates with a small factor.
///
/// \return Pointer to the first entry. The pointer is const, although the
///         array itself is not declared const in the source.
/// \see XMP_Fetch_Prime_Size, RemainderTable::RemainderTable,
///      XMP_Small_Divisors_Test

/// \fn int XMP_Fetch_Prime_Size(void)
/// \brief Returns the number of entries in the small prime table, 3,511.
///
/// The count is computed from the array's declared size, so it follows the
/// table if the table changes. In retail its direct callers are the same
/// two RemainderTable members that call XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table.
/// RemainderTable itself keeps a fixed array of 3,511 remainders, so the
/// two sizes have to agree.
///
/// \return The entry count of the table that XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table returns.
/// \see XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table, RemainderTable::Increment

/// \fn void XMP_Init(digit *number, digit value, int precision)
/// \brief Sets a number to a single-digit unsigned value.
///
/// Clears all precision digits, then stores value in the lowest one. Int's
/// two constructors are built on it: Int(void) gives 0 and
/// Int(unsigned long) gives the value. Most multiply and divide routines in
/// mpmath.cpp also clear their outputs with it; in retail those uses are
/// compiled inline. The direct callers in retail include both out-of-line
/// Int<64> constructors, the PKey constructors, INIClass::Get_PKey,
/// several Int<64> operators, Int::exp_b_mod_c, Int::Inverse,
/// PKey::Encrypt, PKey::Decrypt, Generate_Prime, PKey::Generate and the
/// startup initializer that zeroes Int<64>'s static Remainder.
///
/// \param number    [out] The number to set.
/// \param value     The value. It is stored as an unsigned digit, so when
///                  precision is above 1 a value such as (digit)-1 gives
///                  4294967295, not -1; XMP_Neg after XMP_Init gives a
///                  negative value.
/// \param precision Digit count of number. At least 1.
/// \see XMP_Move, XMP_Test_Eq_Int

/// \fn void XMP_Move(digit *dest, digit const *source, int precision)
/// \brief Copies one number into another of the same digit count.
///
/// A memcpy of precision digits. The signed routines use it to take
/// working copies before they negate, and XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B,
/// XMP_Prepare_Modulus, XMP_Mod_Mult, XMP_Exponent_Mod, XMP_Fermat_Test and
/// XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test use it to set up and return their scratch
/// numbers. In retail every one of these copies is compiled inline; the
/// out-of-line copy has no caller.
///
/// \param dest      [out] The copy.
/// \param source    The number copied. Must not overlap dest.
/// \param precision Digit count of both.
/// \see XMP_Init

/// \fn void XMP_Inc(digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Adds one to a number in place.
///
/// Increments the lowest digit and carries into the next only while a
/// digit wraps to 0, so it usually touches one digit. The largest unsigned
/// value (every bit set, which is -1 in two's complement) wraps to 0, and
/// nothing reports it. XMP_Neg is XMP_Not followed by XMP_Inc. Its other
/// callers are XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Prepare_Modulus and XMP_Mod_Mult, and
/// int.h builds Int's prefix ++ operator on it.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number. At least 1.
/// \see XMP_Dec, XMP_Neg

/// \fn void XMP_Dec(digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Subtracts one from a number in place.
///
/// The mirror of XMP_Inc: decrements the lowest digit and borrows from the
/// next only while a digit wraps to all ones. 0 becomes -1 (every bit
/// set). XMP_Fermat_Test uses it to form candidate - 1 for its exponent.
/// Its other callers are XMP_Signed_Div, which uses it to round a negative
/// quotient down, and XMP_Mod_Mult, and int.h builds Int's prefix --
/// operator on it.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number. At least 1.
/// \see XMP_Inc, XMP_Fermat_Test

/// \fn void XMP_Not(digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Inverts every bit of a number in place (the one's complement).
///
/// XMP_Neg calls it and then adds one, which gives the two's-complement
/// negation. int.h builds Int's ~ operator on it. That operator complements
/// the object itself and then returns a copy, so it changes its operand,
/// unlike the built-in ~.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \see XMP_Neg, XMP_Abs

/// \fn void XMP_Abs(digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Replaces a negative number with its magnitude, in place.
///
/// Calls XMP_Neg when XMP_Is_Negative reports the sign bit set, and does
/// nothing otherwise. Zero Hour's int.h builds Int's Abs member on it.
/// Like Int's ~ operator, that member changes the object itself as well as
/// returning a copy.
///
/// \param number    [in,out] The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \note The most negative value, with only the sign bit set, has no
///       positive counterpart in two's complement. Negating it gives the
///       same value, so it stays negative.
/// \see XMP_Neg, XMP_Is_Negative

/// \fn int XMP_Significance(const digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Counts a number's significant digits: the position of its
///        highest non-zero digit, plus one.
///
/// Scans down from the top digit and stops at the first that is not 0. A
/// value of 0 gives 0. The number is read as unsigned, so a negative number
/// always gives precision, because its top digit is never 0. Zero Hour's
/// mpmath.cpp comments the function as counting bytes, but the count is in
/// digits.
///
/// Callers use it to skip leading zero digits: XMP_Count_Bits,
/// XMP_Test_Eq_Int, XMP_Is_Small_Prime, XMP_Mod_Mult, and
/// XMP_Prepare_Modulus, which uses it to locate the top two digits of the
/// modulus. XMP_Exponent_Mod uses it to run its whole computation at the
/// modulus's significant length. In retail only XMP_Exponent_Mod calls the
/// out-of-line copy.
///
/// \param number    The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number. At least 1.
/// \return 0 to precision.
/// \see XMP_Count_Bits, XMP_Count_Bytes

/// \fn bool XMP_Test_Eq_Int(digit const *r, int i, int p)
/// \brief Tests whether a number equals a small non-negative integer.
///
/// True when the lowest digit equals i and no higher digit is set, which
/// XMP_Significance checks. The library uses it for its tests against 0
/// and 1: zero-divisor and zero-operand checks in XMP_Unsigned_Mult,
/// XMP_Unsigned_Div, XMP_Reciprocal and XMP_Exponent_Mod, the loop
/// condition of XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B, the remainder test in XMP_Signed_Div,
/// and the "power is 1" test of XMP_Fermat_Test and XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test.
/// int.h builds Int's ! operator on it. In retail the mpmath.cpp calls are
/// compiled inline, and nothing calls the out-of-line copy.
///
/// \param r The number.
/// \param i The integer to compare with, meant to be non-negative (see the
///          note).
/// \param p Digit count of r.
/// \return true if r equals i.
/// \note A negative i is compared as an unsigned digit. When p is above 1
///       it never matches the negative number of the same value, whose
///       higher digits are all set, but it does match the positive number
///       whose low digit has the same bits: i = -1 matches 4294967295.
/// \see XMP_Significance, XMP_Compare

/// \fn bool XMP_Is_Negative(const digit *r, int precision)
/// \brief Tests the sign bit, the top bit of a number's highest digit.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h. XMP_Signed_Mult_Int, XMP_Signed_Mult and
/// XMP_Signed_Div use it to decide whether to work on a negated copy of an
/// operand. XMP_Abs uses it to decide whether to negate its argument in
/// place. XMP_Inverse_A_Mod_B uses it to bring a negative result into range
/// by adding the modulus. XMP_Encode and XMP_Encode_Bounded use it to pick
/// the sign byte, and int.h builds Int's Is_Negative member on it. In
/// retail the mpmath.cpp calls are compiled inline, and nothing calls the
/// out-of-line copy.
///
/// \param r         The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of r. The sign is read from digit
///                  precision - 1, so the count must be the number's real
///                  width.
/// \return true if the sign bit is set.
/// \see XMP_Abs, XMP_Neg

/// \fn int XMP_Count_Bytes(const digit *number, int precision)
/// \brief Meant to count the bytes a number needs. As written, it counts
///        the non-zero bytes at the low end of the number, up to the first
///        zero byte.
///
/// Zero Hour's mpmath.cpp comments the function as giving the number's
/// "byte precision", the bytes needed to represent it. The body instead
/// walks the bytes from the least significant upwards and stops at the
/// first zero byte. So a one-digit number whose four bytes are all non-zero
/// gives 4, but 256 gives 0 (its lowest byte is zero) and 65537 gives 1
/// (its bytes from the lowest are 01, 00, 01). It matches the
/// description only for numbers with no zero byte below their top byte.
/// For a byte-count measure, XMP_Count_Bits rounded up to whole bytes is
/// the reliable route.
///
/// int.h builds Int's ByteCount member on it. No code in mpmath.cpp calls
/// it, and nothing in the retail image calls the out-of-line copy.
///
/// \param number    The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of number.
/// \return 0 to precision * 4.
/// \see XMP_Significance, XMP_Count_Bits

/// \fn int XMP_Digits_To_Bits(int digits)
/// \brief Converts a digit count to a bit count: digits times 32.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h; it shifts left by LOG_UNITSIZE. XMP_Count_Bits
/// uses it to turn the significant digit count into an upper bound before
/// it looks for the top set bit. In retail that use is compiled inline;
/// the out-of-line copy has no caller.
///
/// \param digits Number of digits.
/// \return digits * 32.
/// \see XMP_Bits_To_Digits, XMP_Count_Bits

/// \fn int XMP_Bits_To_Digits(int bits)
/// \brief Converts a bit count to the number of digits that hold it,
///        rounding up.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h: (bits + 31) / 32, so 1 to 32 bits give 1 digit and
/// 33 give 2. The routines that walk a number bit by bit from its top set
/// bit (XMP_Unsigned_Mult, XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int, XMP_Unsigned_Div and
/// XMP_Exponent_Mod) use it, with XMP_Bits_To_Mask, to find the digit and
/// the bit they start from. XMP_Reciprocal instead passes the divisor's
/// bit count plus one, so its loop starts one bit above the divisor's top
/// set bit, at the top bit of its quotient. In retail all of these uses
/// are compiled inline; the out-of-line copy has no caller.
/// XMP_Randomize also uses it in a debug-build assert that the requested
/// bits fit in MAX_UNIT_PRECISION digits.
///
/// \param bits Number of bits. Not negative.
/// \return The digit count.
/// \see XMP_Digits_To_Bits, XMP_Bits_To_Mask

/// \fn digit XMP_Bits_To_Mask(int bits)
/// \brief Returns the single-bit mask of the highest bit of a bits-wide
///        number within its top digit.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h: 1 shifted left by (bits - 1) modulo 32, or 0 when
/// bits is 0. Despite the name, the result has exactly one bit set; it is
/// not a mask of the low bits. Paired with XMP_Bits_To_Digits it gives the
/// digit and bit where a number's top set bit lives, which is where
/// XMP_Unsigned_Mult, XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int, XMP_Unsigned_Div and
/// XMP_Exponent_Mod start their bit-by-bit loops. XMP_Reciprocal passes
/// the divisor's bit count plus one, to start one bit higher, at the top
/// bit of its quotient. In retail only XMP_Exponent_Mod calls the
/// out-of-line copy.
///
/// \param bits Bit count of the number, usually from XMP_Count_Bits.
/// \return The mask, or 0 for a bit count of 0.
/// \see XMP_Bits_To_Digits, XMP_Count_Bits, XMP_Exponent_Mod

/// \fn void XMP_Set_Bit(digit *r, unsigned bit)
/// \brief Sets one bit of a number, counting from bit 0 of the lowest
///        digit.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h. The digit is bit / 32 and the bit within it is
/// bit % 32. XMP_Reciprocal uses it to seed its running remainder with
/// the bit at the divisor's top bit position, and int.h builds Int's
/// Set_Bit member on it. In retail the use in
/// XMP_Reciprocal is compiled inline; the out-of-line copy has no caller.
///
/// \param r   [in,out] The number.
/// \param bit Index of the bit to set.
/// \warning There is no precision argument and no bounds check. A bit
///          index at or above the number's width writes past its end.
/// \see XMP_Test_Bit

/// \fn bool XMP_Test_Bit(const digit *r, unsigned bit)
/// \brief Reads one bit of a number, counting from bit 0 of the lowest
///        digit.
///
/// Inline in mpmath.h, with the same indexing as XMP_Set_Bit.
/// XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test uses it to find the lowest set bit of w - 1, and
/// int.h builds Int's [] operator on it. In retail that use is compiled
/// inline; the out-of-line copy has no caller.
///
/// \param r   The number.
/// \param bit Index of the bit to read.
/// \return true if the bit is set.
/// \warning As with XMP_Set_Bit there is no bounds check; an index at or
///          above the number's width reads past its end.
/// \see XMP_Set_Bit

/// \fn static int _Byte_Precision(unsigned long value)
/// \brief Counts the significant bytes of a 32-bit value.
///
/// Returns the position of the highest non-zero byte plus one: 0 for 0,
/// 1 for 1 to 255, 2 for 256 to 65535, 3 below 2 to the power 24, and 4
/// from 2 to the power 24 upwards. XMP_DER_Length_Encode uses it twice for
/// a long-form DER length: once for the byte count it ORs with 0x80 into
/// the first length byte, and once to drive the loop that writes the
/// length bytes after it. In retail both uses are compiled inline;
/// the out-of-line copy has no caller.
///
/// \param value The value.
/// \return 0 to 4.
/// \note The comment on the function in Zero Hour's mpmath.cpp gives 1 as
///       the minimum, but a value of 0 returns 0. XMP_DER_Length_Encode
///       calls it only for lengths above 127, so this does not arise
///       there.
/// \see XMP_DER_Length_Encode

/// \fn void memrev(char *buffer, size_t length)
/// \brief Reverses the order of the bytes in a buffer, in place.
///
/// Swaps bytes from both ends towards the middle. It would turn a
/// little-endian byte string into a big-endian one, but nothing uses it:
/// mpmath.cpp declares and defines it without calling it, Zero Hour's
/// WWLib sha.cpp only declares it, and the retail image holds no call to
/// it and no stored address of it.
///
/// \param buffer [in,out] The bytes to reverse.
/// \param length Number of bytes. 0 and 1 leave the buffer unchanged.
/// \see XMP_Encode, XMP_Signed_Decode

/// \fn int pfunc(const void *pkey, const void *base)
/// \brief The bsearch comparison function for the small prime table:
///        compares two unsigned 16-bit values.
///
/// Returns -1, 1 or 0 as the value at pkey is below, above or equal to the
/// table entry at base. XMP_Is_Small_Prime is its only user: its address is
/// passed to bsearch there, the only reference to it in the retail image.
/// Because XMP_Is_Small_Prime passes the address of its candidate pointer
/// as the key, the 16 bits read through pkey are the low half of that
/// pointer, not the number; XMP_Is_Small_Prime's entry describes the
/// effect.
///
/// \param pkey Pointer to the key, read as an unsigned short.
/// \param base Pointer to a table entry.
/// \return -1, 0 or 1.
/// \see XMP_Is_Small_Prime, XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table
